::: {.doc-subject}

Measure how much one contact constrains a node (Burt's local constraint).
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L54916-L55011){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.local_constraint(
    node_1,
    node_2,
    weighted=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Local constraint](../assets/graphs/local-constraint.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="The shared seven-node graph with the queried pair A and B outlined. A puts half its ties into B directly (0.50) and reaches B again through their mutual contact C, drawn in accent: A's half share in C times C's quarter share in B adds 0.125. The card squares the sum, (0.50 + 0.125)² = 0.39." width="100%"}

Burt's constraint (see `constraint()`) is a sum over a node's contacts; local constraint
is one term of that sum: how much `node_2` limits `node_1`'s room to maneuver. It is
`(p_12 + Σ_k p_1k p_k2)^2`, where `p_ij` is the share of `i`'s ties invested in `j`: the
direct investment in `node_2` plus the indirect investment through mutual contacts `k`.
A contact that `node_1` relies on heavily, directly and through everyone else it knows,
is highly constraining.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[node_1]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The node whose constraint is measured, as an integer ID or a string label.

<code>[node_2]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The contact, as an integer ID or a string label.

<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Whether to use edge weights as tie strengths. If `None` (default), follows the graph.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The local constraint of `node_1` with respect to `node_2`.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If a node does not exist.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~constraint`) 

:   The total constraint on a node.

[](`~effective_size`) 

:   The number of non-redundant neighbors.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Summing local constraint over a node's contacts gives its overall `constraint()`:

::: {#fa58a5ad .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("A", "C"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "D")]
)

parts = [graph.local_constraint("A", other) for other in ["B", "C"]]

total = graph.constraint(return_dict=True)[graph.get_id_from_label("A")]

round(sum(parts), 4), round(total, 4)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(1.0069, 1.0069)
```
:::
:::


### Which in-law constrains the Strozzi?

A contact constrains a node more when the node invests a large share of its ties in it,
directly or through the node's other contacts. Each of the Strozzi's four in-laws gets a
quarter of the family's ties, but the Peruzzi also married into two of the others, so
they constrain the Strozzi the most, and the Ridolfi, who married none of them, the
least:

::: {#4e2dbe93 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
florentine = ec.florentine_families()

in_laws = ["Castellani", "Peruzzi", "Bischeri", "Ridolfi"]

shares = {
    family: florentine.local_constraint("Strozzi", family)
    for family in in_laws
}
shares
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
{'Castellani': 0.1111111111111111,
 'Peruzzi': 0.17361111111111108,
 'Bischeri': 0.1111111111111111,
 'Ridolfi': 0.0625}
```
:::
:::


Coloring the Strozzi's ego network by each in-law's share puts the Peruzzi, at the
center of the closed triangles, darkest:

::: {#42ee1c81 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
(
    florentine.ego_graph("Strozzi")
    .set_node_attrs("local_constraint", shares)
    .plot(color_by="local_constraint", label_by="label", seed=1)
)
```

